  "Question": "If \\(f(2x)=\\frac{2}{2+x}\\) for all \\(x\\gt0,\\) then \\(2f(x)=\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\frac{2}{1+x}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\frac{2}{2+x}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\frac{4}{1+x}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\frac{4}{2+x}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\frac{8}{4+x}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "To change \\(f(2x)\\) into \\(f(x)\\), substitute \\(x/2\\) for the input variable in the given identity. For \\(x>0\\), we also have \\(x/2>0\\), so the identity applies:\n\\[\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{2+x/2}=\\frac{4}{4+x}.\n\\]\nMultiplying by \\(2\\) now gives\n\\[\n\\boxed{2f(x)=\\frac{8}{4+x}},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\). The denominator is positive for every \\(x>0\\).",
